{"text": "Aaliyah learns most successfully through accessible digital copies before class; tactile or enlarged diagrams; verbalizing visual patterns; and extra visual-rest time. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Aaliyah may struggle with glare and low contrast; copying from the board; small labels and crowded worksheets; and fast ball games and unfamiliar routes. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-E-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Aaliyah", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.5625, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 7}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.575, "end": 0.95, "start_char": 8, "end_char": 14}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.9625, "end": 1.225, "start_char": 15, "end_char": 19}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.2375, "end": 1.9125, "start_char": 20, "end_char": 32}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.925, "end": 2.15, "start_char": 33, "end_char": 40}, {"text": "accessible", "start": 2.1625, "end": 2.7625, "start_char": 41, "end_char": 51}, {"text": "digital", "start": 2.775, "end": 3.175, "start_char": 52, "end_char": 59}, {"text": "copies", "start": 3.1875, "end": 3.625, "start_char": 60, "end_char": 66}, {"text": "before", "start": 3.6375, "end": 3.9375, "start_char": 67, "end_char": 73